  "query_preview": "..(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 430 AM EST SUN NOV 15 2026\n\nA pronounced ridging pattern looks to dominate the long term \nforecast period, leading to seasonably tranquil sensible weather \nconditions across all of Eastern Kentucky through at least next \nSaturday. Forecast confidence is high in this time frame, as members \nof the guidance suite collectively resolve the amplification, \neastward propagation, and then suppression of this feature along \nsimilar timelines. At the beginning of the period, the ridge axis \nwill be positioned immediately upstream of the forecast area. The \nrelated synoptic-scale subsidence will reinforce the attendant \nsurface high pressure system and keep it in the proximity of the JKL \nCWA for most of the period. Such a setup yields mostly clear skies \nand efficient diurnal heating/cooling/mixing processes through \nmidweek, when the ridge should reach its peak intensity. Mean LREF \n500mb geopotential height values climb towards the 98th percentile \nof model climatology over the Ohio River Valley on Wednesday \nafternoon, but a shortwave trough passing through the Upper Midwest \nlooks to suppress/flatten the ridge on Thursday/Friday. The ridge \naxis accordingly shifts to the east-southeast of the CWA through \nSaturday, which, in turn, will allow the flow throughout the column \nto adopt more southerly and westerly directional components. The \nresultant warm air advection should yield a noticeable week-end \nwarming trend, but the ridge's continued proximity will keep the \nairmass dry. So, expect fair skies and dry conditions to persist \nthrough the end of the forecast period.    \n\nGiven all of the above, the only notable deviations from the \nbaseline NBM guidance used to populate the long term forecast grids \nremain the addition of overnight ridge-valley temperature splits. \nLight pressure gradient winds and mostly clear skies should easily \nallow the valleys to decouple after sunset each night. As the colder \nair pools into these lower elevation locales on Monday, Tuesday, and \nWednesday nights, temperatures should easily drop down below the \nfreezing mark. The conventionally-coldest sheltered and shaded \nhollows could drop down into the 20s each night, but MOS guidance \nsuggests that the ridgetops could remain above freezing. The coldest \ntemperatures are forecast for Monday night/Tuesday morning, when the \nsurface high will be closest to the CWA. NBM ensemble probabilities \nfor MinTs less than or equal to 25 degrees peak in the 40-60% range \nin this time frame, but these probabilities decrease to less than \n10% area-wide on Thursday morning. As the ridging collectively \nshifts east and the aforementioned warming trend comes to fruition, \nit will intrinsically become harder for overnight lows to cool off \nas much. High temperatures should moderate back into the 60s area-\nwide on Wednesday, then continue climbing towards the low 70s \nthrough Saturday. Expect dewpoints to remain in the 30s though, with \nthe exact value of mixed afternoon dews playing a role in how cold \nit gets at night. The coldest valleys could still reach frost/freeze \ntemperatures at night, so it might be a good idea to keep window \nscrapers ready if you have to park outside at night. Other than this \nfrost potential though, very little weather-related societal impacts \nare anticipated across Eastern Kentucky between Tuesday and \nSaturday.",
